<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="nagata-filling">
  <title>Interior lattice points and interpolation</title>
  <section xml:id="nagata-filling-interior">
    <title>Interior lattice points</title>
    <p>The counting argument in <xref ref="bib-openai-higher" detail="Lemma 5.1"/> and <xref ref="bib-openai-positive" detail="Lemma 5.1"/> uses addition across degrees to determine which lattice points occur in the interior of a bounding convex set. For a more general approximation theorem for additive semigroups, see also <xref ref="bib-kaveh-khovanskii" detail="Theorem 1.6"/>.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-interior-filling">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>P\subset\mathbb R^n</m>, with <m>n\geq1</m>, be compact and convex with nonempty interior. Suppose that finite sets <m>F_N\subset NP\cap\mathbb Z^n</m>, indexed by all integers <m>N\geq1</m>, satisfy<md number="no">F_N+F_{N'}\subset F_{N+N'}\qquad(N,N'\geq1)</md>and<md number="no">\#F_N=\operatorname{vol}(P)N^n+o(N^n).</md>For every compact set <m>K\subset\operatorname{int}P</m>, there is an integer <m>N_K</m> such that<md number="no">NK\cap\mathbb Z^n\subset F_N\qquad(N\geq N_K).</md></p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>The boundary of a full-dimensional compact convex set has measure zero. Lattice-point counting by Riemann sums gives<md number="no">\#(NP\cap\mathbb Z^n)=\operatorname{vol}(P)N^n+o(N^n).</md>Thus the sets of missing lattice points<md number="no">H_N=(NP\cap\mathbb Z^n)\setminus F_N</md>satisfy <m>\#H_N=o(N^n)</m>.</p>
        <p>Assume <m>K</m> is nonempty. Choose <m>\rho&gt;0</m> so that the closed ball of radius <m>\rho</m> about each point of <m>K</m> lies in <m>P</m>. For a large integer <m>N</m>, let <m>N_1=\lfloor N/2\rfloor</m> and <m>N_2=N-N_1</m>. Fix <m>y\in NK\cap\mathbb Z^n</m> and write <m>z=y/N</m>. Every lattice vector <m>y_1</m> satisfying<md number="no">\|y_1-N_1z\|\leq\rho\min(N_1,N_2)</md>defines a decomposition <m>y=y_1+y_2</m> with <m>y_i\in N_iP\cap\mathbb Z^n</m>. Indeed, <m>y_1/N_1</m> and <m>y_2/N_2</m> both lie within distance <m>\rho</m> of <m>z</m>.</p>
        <p xml:id="anchor-interior-decompositions">The ball from which <m>y_1</m> is chosen contains at least <m>cN^n</m> lattice points for some <m>c&gt;0</m> and all sufficiently large <m>N</m>, uniformly in its center. An inscribed cube gives this estimate. At most <m>\#H_{N_1}</m> choices have <m>y_1\notin F_{N_1}</m>. At most <m>\#H_{N_2}</m> choices have <m>y_2\notin F_{N_2}</m>, since <m>y_1\mapsto y-y_1</m> is injective. Their sum is <m>o(N^n)</m>, so one decomposition has <m>y_i\in F_{N_i}</m>. Addition across degrees gives <m>y\in F_N</m>. The bounds are uniform over <m>y\in NK\cap\mathbb Z^n</m>, which proves the assertion.</p>
      <aside xml:id="margin-interior-decompositions"><p>A leading lattice-point count alone permits isolated holes in every degree. An interior point here has at least <m>cN^n</m> possible decompositions, whereas only <m>o(N^n)</m> use a missing summand. The addition property then forces the point itself into <m>F_N</m>.</p></aside>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="nagata-filling-interpolation">
    <title>Interpolation in an elongated simplex</title>
    <p>The interpolation argument in <xref ref="bib-openai-higher" detail="Lemma 5.2"/> and <xref ref="bib-openai-positive" detail="Lemma 5.2"/> uses the Chinese remainder theorem in the last coordinate.</p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-elongated-jets">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>n,r\geq1</m> and <m>\ell\geq0</m> be integers. Let <m>c_1,\ldots,c_r\in k^\times</m> be distinct, and define<md number="no">E_{r,\ell}=\left\{(\beta,b)\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}^{n-1}\times\mathbb Z_{\geq0}:|\beta|+\frac br&lt;\ell+1\right\},\qquad |\beta|=\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\beta_j.</md>Then <m>\mathcal M(E_{r,\ell})</m> separates jets through degree <m>\ell</m> at<md number="no">q_i=(1,\ldots,1,c_i)\in\mathbb T_k^n,\qquad 1\leq i\leq r.</md>For <m>n=1</m>, the tuple <m>\beta</m> is empty and <m>|\beta|=0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Write the coordinates as <m>(x',Y)</m>, with <m>x'=(x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1})</m>. A prescribed jet through degree <m>\ell</m> at <m>q_i</m> has a unique expression<md number="no">\sum_{|\alpha|\leq\ell}(x'-1)^\alpha h_{i,\alpha}(Y-c_i),\qquad\deg h_{i,\alpha}\leq\ell-|\alpha|.</md>For each <m>\alpha</m>, the ideals <m>(Y-c_i)^{\ell+1-|\alpha|}</m> are pairwise comaximal. Their product is generated by a monic polynomial of degree <m>r(\ell+1-|\alpha|)</m>. The Chinese remainder theorem therefore gives a polynomial <m>f_\alpha(Y)</m> such that<md number="no">f_\alpha(Y)\equiv h_{i,\alpha}(Y-c_i)\pmod{(Y-c_i)^{\ell+1-|\alpha|}}\qquad(1\leq i\leq r)</md>and <m>\deg f_\alpha&lt;r(\ell+1-|\alpha|)</m>.</p>
        <p>The polynomial<md number="no">f(x',Y)=\sum_{|\alpha|\leq\ell}(x'-1)^\alpha f_\alpha(Y)</md>has all the prescribed jets. Every monomial <m>(x')^\beta Y^b</m> in its <m>\alpha</m>-summand satisfies <m>\beta_j\leq\alpha_j</m> for every <m>j</m> and <m>b&lt;r(\ell+1-|\alpha|)</m>. Hence<md number="no">|\beta|+\frac br\leq|\alpha|+\frac br&lt;\ell+1.</md>Thus <m>f\in\mathcal M(E_{r,\ell})</m>. The proof uses coefficients of powers of local parameters, so it applies in every characteristic.</p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="nagata-filling-jets">
    <title>Jets from additive exponent sets</title>
    <p>A smaller simplex translated into the interior allows <xref ref="lem-interior-filling"/> to be applied to the exponents in <xref ref="lem-elongated-jets"/>. This is <xref ref="bib-openai-higher" detail="Proposition 5.3"/> and the interpolation part of <xref ref="bib-openai-positive" detail="Proposition 5.3"/>.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-full-volume-jets">
      <statement>
        <p>Let <m>n\geq2</m> and <m>r\geq1</m> be integers, let <m>w&gt;0</m>, and let<md number="no">P=\Delta(w,\ldots,w,rw).</md>Suppose that finite sets <m>F_N\subset NP\cap\mathbb Z^n</m>, for every integer <m>N\geq1</m>, satisfy<md number="no">F_N+F_{N'}\subset F_{N+N'}\qquad(N,N'\geq1)</md>and<md number="no">\#F_N=\operatorname{vol}(P)N^n+o(N^n).</md>For every real <m>0&lt;\theta&lt;w</m> and every choice of distinct <m>c_1,\ldots,c_r\in k^\times</m>, the space <m>\mathcal M(F_N)</m> separates jets through degree <m>\lfloor N\theta\rfloor</m> at the points <m>(1,\ldots,1,c_i)</m> for all sufficiently large <m>N</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p xml:id="anchor-translated-simplex">Choose <m>\lambda</m> with <m>\theta/w&lt;\lambda&lt;1</m>. Choose a vector <m>a\in\mathbb R_{&gt;0}^n</m> satisfying<md number="no">\frac{a_1+\cdots+a_{n-1}}w+\frac{a_n}{rw}&lt;1-\lambda.</md>Then <m>a+\lambda P\subset\operatorname{int}P</m>. Choose <m>a_N\in N^{-1}\mathbb Z^n</m> with <m>a_N\to a</m>. For large <m>N</m>, all translated simplices <m>a_N+\lambda P</m> lie in a single compact subset <m>K</m> of <m>\operatorname{int}P</m>. By <xref ref="lem-interior-filling"/>,<md number="no">Na_N+(N\lambda P\cap\mathbb Z^n)\subset F_N</md>for all sufficiently large <m>N</m>.</p>
      <aside xml:id="margin-translated-simplex"><p>Contraction and positive translation place the interpolation simplex in a compact subset of the interior. The interior-filling lemma applies there. The translation becomes a Laurent monomial factor. This factor is a unit at every torus point, so it gives an automorphism of each jet algebra.</p></aside>
        <p>Let <m>\ell=\lfloor N\theta\rfloor</m>. Since <m>\theta&lt;\lambda w</m>, we have <m>\ell+1\leq N\lambda w</m> for large <m>N</m>. For every <m>(\beta,b)\in E_{r,\ell}</m>,<md number="no">\frac{|\beta|}{\lambda w}+\frac{b}{r\lambda w}&lt;\frac{\ell+1}{\lambda w}\leq N.</md>Thus <m>E_{r,\ell}\subset N\lambda P\cap\mathbb Z^n</m>, and <xref ref="lem-elongated-jets"/> proves jet separation by the monomials with these exponents. Multiplication by the Laurent monomial of exponent <m>Na_N\in\mathbb Z^n</m> induces an isomorphism of each jet algebra at a torus point, because this monomial is a unit there. The translated monomials belong to <m>\mathcal M(F_N)</m>, which proves the required surjectivity.</p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </section>
</chapter>
